    kg:textSpan "An Empirical Exploration of Recurrent Network Architectures Rafal Jozefowicz RAFALJ @ GOOGLE . COM Google Inc. Wojciech Zaremba WOJ . ZAREMBA @ GMAIL . COM New York University, Facebook 1 Ilya Sutskever ILYASU @ GOOGLE . COM Google Inc. Abstract train due to the exploding and the vanishing gradient problems ( Hochreiter , 1991 ; Bengio et al. , 1994 ). The Recurrent Neural Network (RNN) is an extremely powerful sequence model that is often difcult to train. The Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) is a" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/jozefowicz15_rag__chunk_001> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "jozefowicz15_rag::chunk_001" ;
    kg:docId "jozefowicz15_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/jozefowicz15_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "Network (RNN) is a DNN that is adapted to sequence data, and as a result the RNN is also extremely expressive. RNNs maintain a vector of activations for each timestep, which makes the RNN extremely deep. Their depth, in turn, makes them difcult to Motivated by this criticism, we attempted to determine whether the LSTM architecture is optimal by means of an extensive evolutionary architecture search. We found specic architectures similar to the Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) ( Cho et al. , 2014 ) tha" .

