    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1504_08083_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "zero mean and unit variance. All experiments use = 1 . We note that [ 6 ] uses a related loss to train a classagnostic object proposal network. Different from our approach, [ 6 ] advocates for a two-network system that separates localization and classication. OverFeat [ 19 ], R-CNN [ 9 ], and SPPnet [ 11 ] also train classiers and bounding-box localizers, however these methods use stage-wise training, which we show is suboptimal for Fast R-CNN (Section 5.1 ). Mini-batch sampling. During ne-tunin" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1506_00019_rag__chunk_011> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "1506.00019_rag::chunk_011" ;
    kg:docId "1506.00019_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1506_00019_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "be dicult. Even for standard feedforward networks, the optimization task is NP-complete Blum and Rivest [1993]. But learning with recurrent networks can be especially challenging due to the diculty of learning long-range dependencies, as described by Bengio et al. [1994] and expanded upon by Hochreiter et al. [2001]. The problems of vanishing and exploding gradients occur when backpropagating errors across many time steps. As a toy example, consider a network with a single input node, a single o" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1506_01497v3_rag__chunk_001> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "1506.01497v3_rag::chunk_001" ;
    kg:docId "1506.01497v3_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1506_01497v3_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "proposals. RPNs are designed to efciently predict region proposals with a wide range of scales and aspect ratios. In contrast to prevalent methods [8], [9], [1], [2] that use Recent advances in object detection are driven by the success of region proposal methods ( e.g ., [4]) and region-based convolutional neural networks (RCNNs) [5]. Although region-based CNNs were computationally expensive as originally developed in [5], their cost has been drastically reduced thanks to sharing convolutions a" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1506_01497v3_rag__chunk_004> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "1506.01497v3_rag::chunk_004" ;
    kg:docId "1506.01497v3_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1506_01497v3_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "simultaneously predicts multiple class-agnostic boxes, generalizing the singlebox fashion of OverFeat. These class-agnostic boxes are used as proposals for R-CNN [5]. The MultiBox proposal network is applied on a single image crop or multiple large image crops ( e.g ., 224 224), in contrast to our fully convolutional scheme. MultiBox does not share features between the proposal and detection networks. We discuss OverFeat and MultiBox in more depth later in context with our method. Concurrent wit" .

